/**
 * Fleet autopilot for one company: one ShipAgent per ship, a shared World, lane assignment with
 * reservations (so ships spread across charters and sources instead of piling onto one), cash moved
 * between banking regions on demand, and automatic ship purchases.
 *
 * Buying: wherever we have presence at a shipyard (a ship there, or a Resident Factor), quote every
 * allowed hull class, score each by payback hours on its best lane net of the extra fleet upkeep,
 * and build the quickest payback within the limits. With no presence at any yard, it prices a build
 * at the yard nearest the fleet's work from the last quotes seen; if one would pay back (counting the
 * fee), it hires a Resident Factor there for the build and dismisses it straight after, so building
 * never waits for a ship to happen by and no factor salary runs on. As the expansion tier nears
 * completion (and for a while after it turns over) part of the spare cash is kept back for the new
 * tier's charters, which nobody can reach at first, so they stall and re-post richer.
 *
 * Guns: every gun takes 2 t of hold. With the "auto" policy a ship calling at a yard is refitted to
 * the count that earns most on its lane: hold space against the cargo pirates are expected to take
 * there (live zone piracy). In phase-1 waters that is usually none.
 *
 * Cash: one lock for the whole company, so "raise cash, then spend it" never interleaves between
 * ships. The home region keeps a reserve sized to the fleet's daily upkeep (bills come from it).
 * A load is planned on the cash spare when its ship reaches the source (cashFlows/cashAt: our other
 * ships' loads bought first, a share of what our cargo at sea is due to be paid before then), planned
 * again there just before buying (resizeLane), and never spends what another ship's load counts on.
 *
 * Loans by quote (finance.ts; runFinance): with leverage on, a loan bridges a hull or a load cash can't fund only when
 * it earns minLoanReturnX its interest and pays back within half its term, within maxDebtPct of net worth, on the
 * Goldsmiths' instant quote against a ship of ours; a request posted alongside lets a player lender, or a bank's lending line
 * (guide 3.13.8: standing terms that quote at once; the request is shaped to fit the cheapest that covers it), refinance it for
 * less. What is owed is kept back (debtFence; outflows on the cash timeline for loads, installments included), repaid
 * early when no ship waits for cash and always in full 2 game days before maturity. A turnkey hull cash can't fund may
 * instead be built on a ship mortgage (mortgages; guide 3.4: amortised over 180 game days, one at a time), never repaid
 * early: only its next installments are kept back. Spare cash the fleet hasn't needed
 * for a while is quoted on players' secured requests from borrowers with a clean record, and withdrawn when it is needed.
 *
 * Ship deals (deals.ts; shipDeals): the buyer also weighs ships for sale at our quays against building their class
 * (buyListing), and commissions at our yards are built to order when the price beats our cheapest build by a clear
 * margin (runCommissions), delivered at once; the Admiralty's (guide 3.4: the Crown pays, the hull joins the Royal Navy) the
 * same way, one taken at a time. Envelopes (runEnvelopes): fresh news at our quays sealed and sold; bought (runEnvelopeBuys, buyEnvelopes)
 * only when their reports' value of information to the next choice of lane beats their price. Hulls ordered from builders (orderHull,
 * reviewHullOrders; orderHulls): the buyer's pick posted as a commission under our own build cost when the wait pays.
 *
 * Remittances (ensureCash) go by bank draft when a bank undercuts the Crown's fee and has the cash there (guide 3.12: the game
 * chooses; never auto-remit): the fee as charged is booked, and what drafts saved is counted. A region short of cash takes our
 * bank deposits there back first (free), or elsewhere when the bank's exchange beats the Crown's fee, else into their own region
 * for the remittance to carry on (pullForRegion).
 *
 * Banks (deposits.ts, bank.ts; runDeposits, runBank; guide 3.13.4): cash the fleet doesn't need, beyond a liquid buffer and the
 * buyer's next hull, goes on demand deposit with healthy banks (insured first, small per bank), pulled back when the fleet needs it
 * or a bank turns unhealthy (one not open is frozen: owed later); counted in net worth and, at healthy banks, as cash every spend
 * may use (spendable, spareWithDeposits): parked idle cash never holds the fleet back nor stops a loan, which covers only what
 * deposits can't. With runBank on (off by default) our own bank is chartered once rich enough, takes no deposits, posts exchange
 * fees under the Crown's for drafts out of surplus vaults and one conservative lending line (its room fenced from deposits, and
 * withdrawn first when a spend needs it); it is never wound down by the autopilot.
 *
 * Passenger voyages (voyages.ts; paxLanes, guide 3.20): the fares of groups waiting where a ship lies (her own port's, cached) count
 * in the lanes she could sail from there, and a passage for fares alone may be planned; the offer is still posted at sail time with
 * one round's wait at most, and one nobody boarded isn't sailed. Commenda ventures (ventures.ts; ventureStart, ventureTurn, guide
 * 3.13.5): a load its source finds cash-limited with no loan planned may raise outside capital instead, our own cash in as a stake,
 * judged again at the buy, sailed alone and settled; one at a time, every call saved before it is sent. Escort fees (escort.ts;
 * escortTerms, escortRequests, guide 3.10): a convoy we form from an armed ship is announced public at our slowest's speed with a fee
 * per ton, our armed ships its escorts; only joiners no slower than us are admitted. Our armed ships also escort other companies'
 * fee-charging convoys bound where they sail anyway (escort-join.ts; escortOtherPick, joinAsEscort, escortStay; escortOthers). Fares
 * and our escort fee are priced by Thompson sampling (bandit.ts; fareBandit), the old nudge its prior and fallback.
 *
 * Components (rnd.ts; fitComponents, guide 3.19): berthed at a yard on her way, a ship is fitted with a component whose readable strengths
 * repay twice its licence, materials, labour and 4% fee over the season left (permanent: never on an insured ship), within a day's budget;
 * research (runRnd, off by default) starts a project only within its budget, makes a disappointing grant public, licenses a good one. News
 * packets (news.ts; runNews, courierTurn, guide 3.11): when Lloyd's envelope desk pays enough, one packet is built under the buyer's rules,
 * sealed, and shuttles between hubs selling the news its boards lack; sold down the broker's ladder when its sales don't cover its
 * upkeep. Ship shares (shares.ts; runShares, guide 3.13.6): only more 64ths of ships we hold, on the earnings our holdings show; ours of a
 * ship that stopped earning listed. Every spend of theirs is gated as the buyer's (avenueCash: spendable, our line, ensureCash).
 *
 * Execution and resting orders (execution.ts; marginalOrders, restingOrders, guide 3.1, 3.2): ships buy and sell order by order against
 * the ladder (agent.ts), splitting a big load across a re-quote when it pays; leftovers rest in our warehouse under a resting ask
 * (restLeftover), a ship bound for her source may get a small resting bid she loads on arrival (restPrebuys, warehouseTake), a confident
 * glut may be bought to sell after it reverts (restGluts); the resting pass reads our orders and fills back, withdraws what is stale
 * (a ship waiting for cash, a changed plan, news, the season's end) and gives back the leases it grew. Every order is saved before it is
 * sent; the resting lock is taken after the cash lock, never before it.
 *
 * Scoring and allocation (allocation.ts; kellySizing, assignByRegret): the planner scores what comes after a leg by the destination's
 * learned terminal value (TerminalValues, fed by every ship's own plan where she lies), judges the best lanes on scenarios and cuts each
 * load to its Kelly/RCK stake on the bankroll (kellyOptions: spare cash, deposits and cargo at sea, less what our loans take; our other
 * ships in the same zones and markets share a cap). Ships asking for lanes together are served by greatest regret (assignBatch), a lane
 * another commit made stale planned again before it is committed, within a time box past which they are served first come as before;
 * a deadline charter the ship in port is about to take goes to a ship of ours at sea after her current leg (a claim on its tons) only
 * when she gains clearly more from it (tryInsertion).
 *
 * The last avenues (charter-posts.ts, freight-issue.ts, insure.ts, futures.ts, monopolies.ts; guide 3.6-3.9, 3.16): a player charter posted
 * at a port of ours where the market model's bid on landing beats the reward the Merchant Adventurers' filler needs by a wide margin
 * (runCharterPosts; postCharters), its deliveries held as `landed` lots and sold through the resting asks; freight issued for goods held
 * where no ship of ours calls for them, to a port of ours bidding clearly better (runFreightIssues; issueFreight); a ship's own voyage
 * insured, at sail time, only when the certainty equivalent of net worth's log-growth says the variance cut beats the premium
 * (insureVoyage; insureLevered); hub futures on the forecast distribution, a quarter of Kelly, forward sales only covered at once
 * (runFutures; futures, off by default); royal monopolies on a strict resale model, resold under resting asks while they stand
 * (runMonopolies; monopolies, off by default). Each spends through avenueCash under the cash lock, then the resting lock, saved before sent.
 *
 * Stalls: for each stalled expansion posting the fleet makes one call (stallStakes): let it lapse for
 * its richer re-post (no ship delivers into it, beyond what fits under the pace line once re-posts showed
 * that safe; loads planned into it that land first wait) when the expected uplift beats what waiting
 * costs, or deliver. A rival's (or our other company's) delivery notice ends a camp at once.
 *
 * Events: a long poll on the company's event log wakes a ship's agent the moment something happens
 * to it (arrival, berth from the queue, eviction, hazard) and refreshes the world as soon as a
 * charter is posted, filled or expires, so idle ships re-plan at once. Public notices (a policy
 * listed, a convoy formed, a laden rival sailing) bring the rival check forward. Timers remain the fallback.
 * World events (shortages, gluts, pirate outbreaks, structural shifts) are read from the public feed
 * with every world refresh; see World.noteEvents.
 *
 * AI (ai/advisor.ts, live only): its setting overrides sit on the user's under hard floors wherever
 * settings are read (ai/directives.ts enforceFloors); its bans go into every plan; its ship orders act
 * through lane assignment and the ship's own decisions; a build order runs the buyer once with every
 * check; its marques and patrols are issued within the marque budget (runAiNaval); a break-up order marks
 * the ship through the dead-weight path's checks and a move order moves home through its hard gates
 * (runAiHousekeeping). What can't be done is reported back to it as a status code, and logged.
 *
 * Progress (each ship's lane and cargo state, sell floors, trips, stats, purchases, the log, the
 * event cursor and the world model's memory) is saved as it changes (journal.ts: MongoDB when
 * MONGODB_URI is set, plus a local file) and picked up again on start, so a restart resumes work.
 *
 * Runs in-process in the Next.js server (fleet-registry.ts) or from the CLI (workers/kibb-fleet.ts).
 */

